A qualified electronic signature is validated by checking a specific set of conditions and by using a validation system that reports correct results and security issues. In particular:

- The validation process must confirm that all of the following were true at the time of signing:  
  - the supporting certificate was a qualified certificate complying with Annex I (E Art. 32.1(a));  
  - the qualified certificate was issued by a qualified trust service provider and was valid (E Art. 32.1(b));  
  - the signature validation data corresponds to the data provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(c));  
  - the unique set of data representing the signatory in the certificate is correctly provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(d));  
  - any use of a pseudonym is clearly indicated to the relying party if a pseudonym was used (E Art. 32.1(e));  
  - the electronic signature was created by a qualified electronic signature creation device (E Art. 32.1(f));  
  - the integrity of the signed data has not been compromised (E Art. 32.1(g));  
  - the requirements provided for in Article 26 were met at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(h)).  
  Note: the content of Article 26 is not provided in the supplied context, so its specific requirements cannot be stated here.

- The validation system must provide the relying party with the correct result of the validation process and allow the relying party to detect any security-relevant issues (E Art. 32.2).

- A qualified validation service may be provided only by a qualified trust service provider that (1) provides validation in compliance with Article 32(1) and (2) allows relying parties to receive the validation result in an automated, reliable, efficient manner, bearing the provider’s advanced electronic signature or seal (E Art. 33.1).

- Recital 57 explains that specifying the components to be assessed by the relying party and the requirements for qualified validation services is intended to ensure legal certainty and make validation easy and convenient at Union level (E Rec. 57).

- A qualified electronic signature based on a qualified certificate issued in one Member State is recognised as a qualified electronic signature in all other Member States (E Art. 25.3).

- A qualified trust service provider must verify the identity (and any specific attributes) of the person to whom the qualified certificate is issued, by appropriate means and in accordance with national law; verification may be done directly or by relying on a third party (E Art. 24).  

- Permitted verification methods (E Art. 24):  
  - (a) by the physical presence of the natural person (or an authorised representative of a legal person);  
  - (b) remotely, using electronic identification means, provided that prior to issuance a physical presence was ensured and those electronic identification means meet the assurance levels "substantial" or "high" set out in Article 8;  
  - (c) by means of a certificate of a qualified electronic signature or of a qualified electronic seal issued in compliance with (a) or (b);  
  - (d) by other identification methods recognised at national level that provide equivalent assurance to physical presence (the equivalent assurance must be confirmed by a conformity assessment body).  

- Additional note: for qualified certificates for electronic seals, trust service providers should implement measures to be able to establish the identity of the natural person representing the legal person when such identification is necessary in judicial or administrative proceedings (E Rec. 60).

- Electronic signatures qualify as "advanced electronic signatures" when they meet all of the following requirements (E Art. 26):
  - they are uniquely linked to the signatory (E Art. 26(a));
  - they are capable of identifying the signatory (E Art. 26(b));
  - they are created using electronic signature creation data that the signatory can, with a high level of confidence, use under his sole control (E Art. 26(c));
  - they are linked to the data signed in such a way that any subsequent change in the data is detectable (E Art. 26(d)).

- Moreover, compliance with those requirements is presumed if the advanced electronic signature meets reference standards established by the Commission by implementing acts (E Art. 27.4).

- General rule: a data protection impact assessment (DPIA) must be carried out by the controller where processing operations are likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons (G Rec. 84; G Rec. 90).

- Examples of situations requiring a DPIA (non‑exhaustive) include:
  - large‑scale processing of a considerable amount of personal data at regional, national or supranational level likely to affect a large number of data subjects (G Rec. 91);
  - large‑scale use of a new technology where, given the state of technological knowledge, a high risk is likely (G Rec. 91);
  - processing that makes it more difficult for data subjects to exercise their rights (G Rec. 91);
  - taking decisions about specific natural persons following systematic and extensive evaluation/profiling (G Rec. 91);
  - processing of special categories of personal data, biometric data, or data on criminal convictions and offences (G Rec. 91);
  - monitoring publicly accessible areas on a large scale, especially using optic‑electronic devices (G Rec. 91);
  - any other operations the competent supervisory authority considers likely to result in a high risk (G Rec. 91).

- Timing and procedure:
  - The DPIA must be carried out prior to the processing (G Rec. 90).
  - If a DPIA indicates that, without safeguards, the processing would result in a high residual risk that cannot be mitigated by reasonable means, the supervisory authority should be consulted prior to the start of processing (G Rec. 94).

- Exception:
  - Processing of personal data from patients or clients by an individual physician, other health care professional or lawyer is not to be considered large scale, and in such cases a DPIA should not be mandatory (G Rec. 91).

- Practical note on conduct:
  - The controller shall seek the advice of the data protection officer, where designated, when carrying out a DPIA (G Art. 35.2).

- Who must keep a record
  - Controllers and, where applicable, their representatives: must maintain a record of processing activities under their responsibility (Art. 30.1).  
  - Processors and, where applicable, their representatives: must maintain a record of all categories of processing activities carried out on behalf of a controller (Art. 30.2).  
  - Such records should be made available to the supervisory authority on request to demonstrate compliance (Rec. 82).

- Minimum contents required for a controller's record (Art. 30.1)
  - name and contact details of the controller and, where applicable, the joint controller, the controller's representative and the data protection officer (Art. 30.1(a));  
  - the purposes of the processing (Art. 30.1(b));  
  - a description of the categories of data subjects and of the categories of personal data (Art. 30.1(c));  
  - the categories of recipients to whom the personal data have been or will be disclosed, including recipients in third countries or international organisations (Art. 30.1(d));  
  - where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including the identification of that third country or international organisation and, in the case of transfers referred to in the second subparagraph of Article 49, where possible, the envisaged time limits for erasure of the different categories of data (Art. 30.1(e));  
  - where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (Art. 30.1(g)).

- Minimum contents required for a processor's record (Art. 30.2)
  - name and contact details of the processor(s) and of each controller on behalf of which the processor is acting, and, where applicable, of the controller's or the processor's representative, and the data protection officer (Art. 30.2(a));  
  - the categories of processing carried out on behalf of each controller (Art. 30.2(b));  
  - where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including the identification of that third country or international organisation and, in the case of transfers referred to in the second subparagraph of Article 49, where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (Art. 30.2(c)–(d)).

- Principles to observe when drafting the record
  - Ensure transparency, using clear and plain language about the controller's identity, purposes and other information necessary for fair and transparent processing (Rec. 39).  
  - Include information enabling limitation of storage and periodic review or erasure time limits so data are not kept longer than necessary (Rec. 39).  
  - Reflect technical and organisational measures and internal policies that demonstrate compliance and data‑protection‑by‑design/by‑default principles (Rec. 78).

(Primary legal requirements: Art. 30.1, Art. 30.2; supporting principles and obligations: Rec. 39, Rec. 78, Rec. 82.)

- General scope: Rome II applies to non‑contractual obligations understood as an autonomous concept and its conflict‑of‑law rules cover non‑contractual obligations arising out of strict liability (RII Rec. 11). It also applies to non‑contractual obligations that are likely to arise (RII Art. 2).

- Exclusions: the following non‑contractual obligations are excluded from the scope of the Regulation (RII Art. 1):
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of family relationships and relationships deemed by the law applicable to such relationships to have comparable effects, including maintenance obligations (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of matrimonial property regimes, property regimes of relationships deemed comparable to marriage, and wills and succession (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising under bills of exchange, cheques and promissory notes and other negotiable instruments insofar as they arise from their negotiable character (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of the law of companies and other bodies corporate or unincorporated concerning creation, legal capacity, internal organisation, winding‑up, personal liability of officers/members for company obligations, and auditors’ personal liability in statutory audits (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of relations between settlors, trustees and beneficiaries of a voluntarily created trust (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of nuclear damage (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of violations of privacy and rights relating to personality, including defamation (RII Art. 1).
